  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1138 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern aloft continues to be relatively unchanged with \nthe deep synoptic low over the Pacific Northwest slowly trekking \neastward and nudging the massive ridging eastward across the \nsoutheastern CONUS. Another weak upper-level low has moved over \nFlorida out of the Atlantic Ocean slowing down the high pressure's \nmovement. With the depth of the synoptic features, the highs and \nlows have associated systems all the way down to the surface with \nhigh pressure in the deep south and a pair of low pressure over \nNevada and South Dakota. \n\nToday will be another hot day warming up well into the 90s and even \npotentially flirting with the triple digit barrier in the warmest \nparts of SW Kansas today. CAMs continue to have storms clipping \nnorthwest Kansas, but the environment over the forecast area is \nsignificantly less conducive for convection. Very little dynamical \nsupport is present aloft outside of an incredibly subtle upper-level \njet streak. The NAM forecast sounding is just as bleak with CAPE \nvalues below 500 J/kg and sfc-3km shear below 20 KTs. If a storm or \ntwo is able to overcome the cap, it expected to be very weak and sub-\nsevere with the best chances placed around the Scott City/Garden \nCity area. With the amount of CAM discrepancies, it is still \nuncertain whether any precipitation will fall today. The current \nprobability for precipitation forecasted is below 10%. Strong \nsouthwest winds are expected to continue today at around 25 mph and \ngusts up to 35 mph. \n\nTomorrow conversely continues to look optimistic for widespread \nprecipitation chances. After another day in the 90s (and around 100 \ndegrees in the southeastern counties) a strong cold front is \nexpected to descend from the larger system and sweep across the CWA \nin the late morning and through the afternoon. The environment is \nconsiderably more favorable for convective development ahead of the \nfront tomorrow afternoon. Mid-level CVA should provide help with \nascent with the same forecast soundings placing CAPE around 2000 \nJ/kg with little to no cap. The two primarily limiters on these \nstorms would be the continued lack of shear (still around 20 KTs) \nand lack of upper-level dynamical support. Despite that, with low \nlevel lapse rates forecast around 10 C/km, wind gusts of 60-65 mph \nis possible in the strongest storms. Hail can not be ruled out but \nis expected to stay very marginal if at all owing to the \naforementioned lack of shear. The linchpin of the forecast is the \ntiming and placement of the front. Days previous model runs had the \nfront near the Highway 56 corridor by mid afternoon. The same models \ntoday (with the NAM being the fastest) have it as far as the Highway \n54 corridor. This may limit the area for potential severe weather to \njust the southeastern 3 or so counties of the CWA. The frontal \nposition will need monitoring into tomorrow. Tomorrow night, benign \nshowers are expected across SW Kansas behind the front. Ensembles \nhave chances from 30-80% with the highest chances in the northern \nzones. With the intensity being forecast as relatively low, the \nensemble means for total accumulations only climb to around 0.4\" \nwith most of SW Kansas seeing it closer to the 0.05-0.1\" range. \n\nBeyond Saturday with the intense CAA behind the front, a significant \npattern shift is expected transitioning from abnormally hot highs \nwith dry weather to a wetter more seasonal pattern. Highs next week \nare forecast to remain steady around the upper 70-lower 80s which is \nright around normal for this time of the year. Every day next week \nhas some rainfall potential via long-ranged ensembles primarily in \nfar western Kansas. Current expectation is that the severe chances \npast Sunday remain very minimal but temporal uncertainty will need \nto decrease before saying so for sure. With the seasonal \ntemperatures and relative humidities quelling fire weather risk by \nstaying above 25%, the only weather headlines expected are regarding \nthe precipitation.",
